%I A074384 #29 Oct 02 2026 10:11:34
%S A074384 2401,9604,21609,28561,38416,60025,86436,114244,130321,153664,194481,
%T A074384 240100,257049,290521,345744,456976,521284,540225,614656,693889,
%U A074384 714025,777924,923521,960400,1028196,1162084,1172889,1270129,1382976,1500625
%N A074384 Solutions to mod(sigma(x), 6) = 5.
%C A074384 From _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 02 2026: (Start)
%C A074384 All the terms are squares. The terms k^2 are fully characterized by the following conditions on the prime factors p == 1 (mod 3) of k:
%C A074384 1) For every prime p == 1 (mod 3) dividing k, the valuation v_p(k) != 1 (mod 3).
%C A074384 2) The number of distinct primes p == 1 (mod 3) with v_p(k) == 2 (mod 3) is odd. (End)
%H A074384 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A074384/b074384.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A074384 {k: A084301(k) = 5}. - _R. J. Mathar_, May 19 2020
%F A074384 Sum_{n>=1} 1/a(n) = (zeta(2)/2) * (Product_{prime p == 1 (mod 3)} ((p^4 + 1)/(p^4 + p^2 + 1)) - Product_{prime p == 1 (mod 3)} ((p^4 - 1)/(p^4 + p^2 + 1))) = 0.000733876748827... . - _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 02 2026
%e A074384 4th powers of primes of the form 6*k+1 are terms because sigma(p^4) = p^4 + p^3 + p^2 + p + 1 congruent to 1+1+1+1+1 = 5 mod 6. There are also other fourth powers, like 38416 = (2*7)^4, 194481 = (3*7)^4, 456976 = (2*13)^4, and solutions which are not fourth powers like 9604 = 2^2*7^4 and 21609 = 3^2*7^4.
%t A074384 Do[s=Mod[DivisorSigma[1, n], 6]; If[s==5, Print[n]], {n, 1, 1000000}]
%t A074384 (* Alternative: *)
%t A074384 Select[Range[1225]^2, Mod[DivisorSigma[1, #], 6] == 5 &] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 02 2026 *)
%o A074384 (PARI) isok(k) = sigma(k) % 6 == 5; \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 02 2026
%o A074384 (PARI) list(kmax) = select(x -> sigma(x) % 6 == 5, vector(sqrtint(kmax), i, i^2)) \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 02 2026
%Y A074384 Cf. A000203, A072862, A072461, A072462.
%Y A074384 Positions of 5's in A084301.
%K A074384 nonn,changed
%O A074384 1,1
